WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.856
A Liquid filter changes a value inside a template, the way slugify turns

00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:08.221
a title into a segment of a URL.

00:00:08.221 --> 00:00:12.828
In this video, you write two filters in a module, register them, and

00:00:12.828 --> 00:00:14.820
call them from a template.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.064
A filter takes the value on the left of the pipe and returns

00:00:23.064 --> 00:00:24.555
a value in its place.

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:29.027
An argument follows a colon, and filters can be chained, each one receiving

00:00:29.027 --> 00:00:30.960
the result of the one before.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.096
Each tenant builds its template options once, the first time it needs them,

00:00:36.096 --> 00:00:38.262
and they are immutable afterwards.

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:43.948
A module adds its filters by configuring the TemplateOptionsBuilder in the ConfigureServices method

00:00:43.948 --> 00:00:45.410
of its Startup class.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.905
The simplest filter is a delegate.

00:00:51.905 --> 00:00:56.666
AddFilter takes the name that templates use, and a function that receives the

00:00:56.666 --> 00:01:00.050
input value, the arguments, and the template context.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.628
Liquid runs on Fluid, and filters work with its values.

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:10.088
Shout reads the input as text with ToStringValue, converts it to capital letters,

00:01:10.088 --> 00:01:13.540
and returns a StringValue, which is a FluidValue.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.784
In a template, shout writes the value that reaches it in capital letters,

00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:21.230
so the text hello comes out as HELLO.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:29.974
A filter that needs services is a class that implements ILiquidFilter.

00:01:29.974 --> 00:01:35.832
Its constructor receives what it needs, here the ISiteService, and ProcessAsync receives the

00:01:35.832 --> 00:01:39.300
input, the arguments, and the LiquidTemplateContext.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:44.935
ProcessAsync is asynchronous, so the filter can await the site settings.

00:01:44.935 --> 00:01:49.319
A positional argument is read with At and its index, a named one

00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:53.641
by its name, and a missing argument is nil, so the filter falls

00:01:53.641 --> 00:01:55.770
back to a default separator.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.683
Register the class with AddLiquidFilter and the name of the filter.

00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:04.961
Orchard Core adds the class as a scoped service, and resolves it from

00:02:04.961 --> 00:02:09.580
the services of the template context each time a template calls the filter.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:15.076
On a site named Orchard Core, the text About piped to with_site_name writes

00:02:15.076 --> 00:02:17.829
About, a vertical bar, and the site name.

00:02:17.829 --> 00:02:19.880
An argument replaces the bar.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.831
Liquid encodes the string a filter returns as HTML.

00:02:27.831 --> 00:02:32.701
To return markup that you trust, create the value with encode set to

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:36.320
false, as the liquid filter of Orchard Core does.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:41.620
When the input can't be used, return NilValue, as the local filter does

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.570
for text that isn't a date.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.520
A nil value writes nothing.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.906
Configure the TemplateOptionsBuilder, never the TemplateOptions.

00:02:49.906 --> 00:02:54.788
A registration that configures the immutable options makes the tenant throw an exception

00:02:54.788 --> 00:02:58.580
when the options are first used, and its message names the builder.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:07.100
The module with both filters is enabled, so every Liquid template of the

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.478
site can call them.

00:03:08.478 --> 00:03:12.997
Under Design, Templates, Add Template opens the editor, and the name of this

00:03:12.997 --> 00:03:15.091
template selects the About page.

00:03:15.556 --> 00:03:20.284
In the Content box, the editor suggests the names of the registered filters,

00:03:20.284 --> 00:03:22.014
so shout is on the list.

00:03:22.014 --> 00:03:24.896
Let's pass the title of the page through it.

00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:29.312
A second expression passes the title to with_site_name.

00:03:29.312 --> 00:03:34.113
Its argument, a dash between spaces, replaces the default separator.

00:03:34.573 --> 00:03:37.551
Save the template, then open the About page.

00:03:37.551 --> 00:03:42.019
The title appears in capital letters, then a slash, then the title, a

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:44.223
dash, and the name of the site.

00:03:48.423 --> 00:03:53.303
The same builder registers global values, value converters, and member access.

00:03:53.303 --> 00:03:56.441
Custom tags are registered on the Liquid parser.

00:03:56.441 --> 00:04:00.043
The Liquid page of the documentation shows each of them.

00:04:02.043 --> 00:04:06.122
A filter is a delegate when it needs nothing and an ILiquidFilter class

00:04:06.122 --> 00:04:10.202
when it needs services, and either way it is registered when the tenant

00:04:10.202 --> 00:04:13.645
starts and every Liquid template of the tenant can call it.

00:04:13.645 --> 00:04:15.023
Thanks for watching.

